{
  "dom_id": "question-131254",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "How many inflection points does the function $f(x)$ have inside the interval $(-6,6),$ and what is the smallest value of $x$ where $f(x)$ has an inflection point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        There are \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \ninflection points in total.\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The inflection point with the smallest $x$-coordinate is at\n\n$x\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ Recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(a)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(a)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nSo, we summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only at the point $x=0.$  Therefore, $x=0$  is a point of inflection.\n\nFinally, the smallest value of $x$ inside the interval $(-6,6),$ where the function $f(x)$ has an inflection point is $x=0.$",
